*The $50,000â$100,000 range is the core mainstream market**, representing nearly three in ten households.\n- **A premium segment is commercially important**, since more than one-third of households earn at least $100,000.\n- **Income targeting should incorporate age**, because household income typically peaks among householders aged 45â64 and falls among younger and older householders.[1]\n- **Education-based segmentation is actionable**, since the supplied data show about **34.3%** of adults aged 25 and over have a bachelorâs or graduate degree, a group more likely to support professional and higher-value offerings.\n- **Lower-income households require distinct pricing**, as nearly four in ten households earn below $50,000.\n- **Geographic and demographic refinement is necessary**, because national averages conceal differences associated with race, ethnicity, age, education, and household composition.\n\n## 6. DISAGREES WITH\n\n- **The median income alone is sufficient for market planning**; the distribution shows substantial household shares both far below and far above the median.\n- **The United States is predominantly a middle-income market**; although middle bands are large, households below $50,000 and at least $100,000 each represent roughly comparable major segments.\n- **All households can be served with one national price point**; the 4.9% below $10,000 and 11.4% at $200,000 or more have sharply different affordability profiles.\n- **Age has little relevance to income strategy**; younger householders have lower median income, while 45â64-year-old householders have the highest median income.[1]\n- **Education should be excluded from segmentation**; attainment varies substantially across the adult population and is closely linked to occupational access and earning potential.\n- **The $100,000-or-more group should be treated as uniformly affluent**; households earning $100,000â$150,000 differ materially from those earning $200,000 or more.\n- **Race and ethnicity can be interpreted as direct causes of income differences**; they should instead be used carefully alongside education, age, geography, occupation, household structure, and labor-force participation to avoid misleading conclusions.",
